@meta
id: 26-12
title: Send the buffer out, change nothing
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: write-shell
par: 26
minutes: 5
vscode: save, switch to the terminal, run wc on the file, switch back
open: pilot-stores.txt

@brief
The comment on line 1 is supposed to end with the number of stores in the
pilot. Somebody removed the number rather than update it. Put it back.

Count the store lines without leaving vim and without counting them
yourself. `:w !cmd` writes lines to a command's standard input the way
`:w file` writes them to a file — the buffer is not touched, nothing is
replaced, the command's output just appears on the screen. It is the read
half of talking to the shell: you ask a question about your text and get
an answer back, and your text stays exactly as it was.

The header is not a store, so do not count it.

@keys
| `:w !cmd` | pipe the whole buffer to a command, buffer unchanged |
| `:{range}w !cmd` | pipe only those lines |
| `:2,$w !wc -l` | how many lines from 2 to the end |
| `!` vs `w !` | `:%!cmd` replaces your lines; `:%w !cmd` shows you an answer |

@hints
The range in `:2,$` is the store lines and nothing else. What goes after
it is a `w`, then a space, then the bang.
--
The output appears at the bottom of the screen and vim waits for Enter
before it will take another key. Read the number, press Enter, then edit
line 1.
--
`:2,$w !wc -l` Enter, Enter, then `A` a space and the number, `Esc`,
`:wq`.

@solution
    :2,$w !wc -l<Enter> <Enter>  A 7<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

The space between `w` and `!` is not optional and it is where everyone
trips. `:w !wc -l` pipes the buffer into `wc`. `:w!wc` is `:w!` — write,
forced — to a file called `wc`, which is a real file you now have to
delete. Same three characters, entirely different day.

Once the distinction is in your fingers, `:w !` is how you interrogate a
buffer without disturbing it: `:w !wc -w` for a word count on a draft,
`:'<,'>w !sort -c` to ask whether a block is already sorted,
`:w !python3 -m json.tool` to find out whether the JSON you are staring
at parses, without reformatting it first. And `:w !bash` runs the buffer
as a script, which is an unnervingly convenient way to test one.

The famous one is worth knowing even though you should not practise it
here:

    :w !sudo tee % > /dev/null

That is the escape hatch for the file you opened, edited for ten minutes
and only then discovered is owned by root. Vim cannot write it — but it
can pipe the buffer to `sudo tee`, and `tee` writes the file with the
privileges vim does not have. The `%` is the current file's name, the
same expansion as in any other `:!` command. Vim then notices the file
changed underneath it and asks; answer `L` to keep your buffer as it is.

@start pilot-stores.txt
# stores in the pilot:
store-aberdeen
store-bristol
store-cardiff
store-dundee
store-exeter
store-glasgow
store-leeds

@expected pilot-stores.txt
# stores in the pilot: 7
store-aberdeen
store-bristol
store-cardiff
store-dundee
store-exeter
store-glasgow
store-leeds
